<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamień Pomorski</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cammin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamień Pomorski, in German Cammin, is a town near the mouth of the Oder in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. From the late 12th century it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, whose Romanesque-Gothic cathedral survives. The bishopric's lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> after the Peace of Westphalia, and the town became Polish in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamien-pomorski.jpg" alt="Kamień Pomorski" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ralf Roletschek · CC BY-SA 1.0 fi · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20-08-09-Kamien-Pomorskie-RalfR-101-0206-Pano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
